{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And he called it El Elohei Yisrael.</b> Not that the altar is called 'Elohei Yisrael,' but because the Holy One, blessed be He, was with him and delivered him, he called the name of the altar after the miracle — so that the praise of the Omnipresent would be recalled in the naming: meaning, He who is El — He is the Holy One, blessed be He — He is my Elohim, whose name is Yisrael. And so we find with Moses: ״וַיִּקְרָא שְׁמוֹ יְהֹוָה נִסִּי״ (Exodus 17) — not that the altar is called YHWH, but he called the name of the altar after the miracle, to recall the praise of the Holy One, blessed be He: YHWH is my banner. And our Sages interpreted that the Holy One, blessed be He, called Yaakov 'El' (Megillah 18); and the words of Torah are like a hammer that shatters rock (cf. Shabbat 8) — they divide into several meanings, but I have come to settle the plain sense of Scripture."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:01:59.529183485Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}